1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,120
Have you ever walked away from

2
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:03,360
a conversation... and somehow ended up

3
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,520
apologizing... even though you weren't sure

4
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:06,480
what you did wrong?

5
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,000
Maybe you tried to explain how

6
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,280
something made you feel.

7
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:11,360
But instead of discussing what happened...

8
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:13,360
the conversation somehow became about your

9
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:15,155
attitude. You were told you were

10
00:00:15,155 --> 00:00:18,035
too sensitive. That you misunderstood everything.

11
00:00:18,275 --> 00:00:20,115
That you always created problems.

12
00:00:20,355 --> 00:00:21,875
And eventually... you stopped trying to

13
00:00:21,875 --> 00:00:24,195
explain yourself. Not because you suddenly

14
00:00:24,195 --> 00:00:25,315
agreed with them.

15
00:00:25,395 --> 00:00:26,675
But because you were exhausted.

16
00:00:27,110 --> 00:00:29,270
You started questioning your own memory.

17
00:00:29,270 --> 00:00:30,390
Your own judgment.

18
00:00:30,550 --> 00:00:32,310
Even your right to feel upset.

19
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:33,670
And the strangest part?

20
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:35,110
The person who hurt you...

21
00:00:35,190 --> 00:00:36,950
somehow became the person you felt

22
00:00:36,950 --> 00:00:38,070
you had to make things right

23
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:40,390
with. Now, not every difficult conversation

24
00:00:40,390 --> 00:00:43,445
is manipulation. People misunderstand each other.

25
00:00:43,605 --> 00:00:44,885
People get defensive.

26
00:00:44,965 --> 00:00:46,245
People make mistakes.

27
00:00:46,405 --> 00:00:48,485
But sometimes... there is a pattern.

28
00:00:48,565 --> 00:00:50,085
A pattern where your feelings are

29
00:00:50,085 --> 00:00:52,725
constantly dismissed. Your boundaries are treated

30
00:00:52,725 --> 00:00:54,965
like betrayal. Your mistakes are remembered

31
00:00:54,965 --> 00:00:57,180
forever... while theirs are somehow never

32
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:59,500
their responsibility. And slowly...

33
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:01,180
you begin changing yourself just to

34
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:02,220
keep the peace.

35
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:03,420
Today, we're going to look at

36
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:05,180
the difference between dark psychological

37
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,420
manipulation and healthy boundaries.

38
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:09,935
We'll explore gaslighting, love-bombing,

39
00:01:09,935 --> 00:01:12,495
controlling behavior, and narcissistic traits.

40
00:01:12,655 --> 00:01:14,415
More importantly... we'll look at how

41
00:01:14,415 --> 00:01:16,735
to recognize unhealthy patterns without assuming

42
00:01:16,735 --> 00:01:18,735
that every disagreement is abuse...

43
00:01:18,815 --> 00:01:20,655
or that every difficult person has

44
00:01:20,655 --> 00:01:21,855
a personality disorder.

45
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:23,770
Because the goal isn't to become

46
00:01:23,770 --> 00:01:25,130
suspicious of everyone.

47
00:01:25,290 --> 00:01:27,370
It's to understand what healthy relationships

48
00:01:27,370 --> 00:01:28,810
should actually feel like.

49
00:01:28,810 --> 00:01:30,490
Let's start with something simple.

50
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:32,010
Imagine you tell someone...

51
00:01:32,170 --> 00:01:33,290
“I didn't like the way you

52
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:34,010
spoke to me earlier.”

53
00:01:34,295 --> 00:01:36,215
In a healthy relationship, the conversation

54
00:01:36,215 --> 00:01:37,495
might be uncomfortable.

55
00:01:37,655 --> 00:01:39,175
They might explain what they meant.

56
00:01:39,175 --> 00:01:41,255
They might disagree with your interpretation.

57
00:01:41,495 --> 00:01:42,375
They might even tell you that

58
00:01:42,375 --> 00:01:43,735
your words hurt them, too.

59
00:01:43,815 --> 00:01:45,735
But ideally... both people can discuss

60
00:01:45,735 --> 00:01:47,335
what happened without one person having

61
00:01:47,335 --> 00:01:48,695
to surrender their perspective just to

62
00:01:48,695 --> 00:01:49,255
end the argument.

63
00:01:49,550 --> 00:01:51,310
Now imagine a different response.

64
00:01:51,470 --> 00:01:52,670
They immediately say...

65
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:54,110
“You're always making me the bad

66
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:56,030
person.” “You're too sensitive.”

67
00:01:56,110 --> 00:01:57,550
“You remember everything wrong.”

68
00:01:57,710 --> 00:01:58,830
“You should be grateful I even

69
00:01:58,830 --> 00:01:59,630
put up with you.”

70
00:01:59,790 --> 00:02:01,790
Suddenly, the original problem disappears.

71
00:02:02,255 --> 00:02:04,015
Now you're defending your character.

72
00:02:04,015 --> 00:02:06,175
You're explaining why you're not ungrateful.

73
00:02:06,175 --> 00:02:07,455
You're trying to prove that you're

74
00:02:07,455 --> 00:02:08,495
not too sensitive.

75
00:02:08,575 --> 00:02:10,335
And the conversation ends without addressing

76
00:02:10,335 --> 00:02:11,375
what upset you in the first

77
00:02:11,375 --> 00:02:13,135
place. Notice what happened.

78
00:02:13,135 --> 00:02:14,095
You brought up a concern.

79
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:16,460
But instead of discussing the concern...

80
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:18,140
you were pushed into defending yourself

81
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:19,180
for having one.

82
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:21,020
If this happens repeatedly, it can

83
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:22,380
become a serious problem.

84
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:24,380
Because healthy communication allows two

85
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:25,500
people to disagree.

86
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:27,340
Manipulative communication can turn

87
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:28,940
disagreement into a way of gaining

88
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,245
control. And understanding that difference is

89
00:02:31,245 --> 00:02:32,125
the first step.

90
00:02:32,125 --> 00:02:33,725
Let's talk about gaslighting.

91
00:02:33,725 --> 00:02:35,085
The word gets used so often

92
00:02:35,085 --> 00:02:36,765
online that it can sometimes lose

93
00:02:36,765 --> 00:02:38,845
its meaning. Gaslighting is more than

94
00:02:38,845 --> 00:02:40,525
someone remembering an event differently from

95
00:02:40,525 --> 00:02:42,445
you. It's not every disagreement about

96
00:02:42,445 --> 00:02:44,130
what happened. And it's not simply

97
00:02:44,130 --> 00:02:45,890
someone denying an accusation.

98
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:48,210
Gaslighting refers to a pattern of

99
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:50,610
manipulation that causes someone to question

100
00:02:50,610 --> 00:02:53,570
their own perception of reality, memory,

101
00:02:53,570 --> 00:02:55,570
or judgment. Imagine you agree to

102
00:02:55,570 --> 00:02:56,530
meet someone at six.

103
00:02:56,865 --> 00:02:58,305
They arrive much later.

104
00:02:58,465 --> 00:02:59,345
When you bring it up, they

105
00:02:59,345 --> 00:03:00,545
tell you that you never agreed

106
00:03:00,545 --> 00:03:02,145
on six in the first place.

107
00:03:02,305 --> 00:03:04,385
You remember the conversation clearly.

108
00:03:04,385 --> 00:03:06,465
But they insist you're imagining things.

109
00:03:06,625 --> 00:03:08,705
That one disagreement alone doesn't prove

110
00:03:08,705 --> 00:03:11,460
gaslighting. Maybe the messages were unclear.

111
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:13,620
Maybe someone genuinely remembers the

112
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:16,020
conversation differently. But imagine this keeps

113
00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:18,260
happening. They deny things they previously

114
00:03:18,260 --> 00:03:21,140
said. They insist conversations never happened.

115
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:22,660
They repeatedly tell you that your

116
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:23,460
memory is terrible.

117
00:03:23,805 --> 00:03:25,805
And whenever you question their behavior...

118
00:03:25,805 --> 00:03:26,765
they make you feel as though

119
00:03:26,765 --> 00:03:28,285
something is wrong with your ability

120
00:03:28,285 --> 00:03:29,645
to understand reality.

121
00:03:29,805 --> 00:03:31,645
Eventually, you start keeping quiet.

122
00:03:31,645 --> 00:03:33,725
You stop trusting your own recollection.

123
00:03:33,885 --> 00:03:35,805
You begin asking them what happened...

124
00:03:35,805 --> 00:03:36,845
even when you were there.

125
00:03:37,190 --> 00:03:38,790
That's the important distinction.

126
00:03:38,950 --> 00:03:40,710
The issue isn't simply that someone

127
00:03:40,710 --> 00:03:42,150
disagrees with your memory.

128
00:03:42,230 --> 00:03:44,390
It's the repeated pattern of denying,

129
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:46,550
distorting, or undermining your reality in

130
00:03:46,550 --> 00:03:47,590
a way that makes you doubt

131
00:03:47,590 --> 00:03:49,670
yourself. So what can you do?

132
00:03:49,910 --> 00:03:52,230
First, don't assume every disagreement is

133
00:03:52,230 --> 00:03:54,525
gaslighting. Look at the broader pattern.

134
00:03:54,685 --> 00:03:56,925
Second, when it's appropriate and safe,

135
00:03:57,005 --> 00:03:58,765
keep track of important agreements and

136
00:03:58,765 --> 00:04:01,245
conversations. Written messages can help you

137
00:04:01,245 --> 00:04:03,325
clarify what was actually agreed upon.

138
00:04:03,405 --> 00:04:05,325
And third... pay attention to whether

139
00:04:05,325 --> 00:04:07,005
the other person can acknowledge evidence.

140
00:04:07,430 --> 00:04:08,950
Can they say, “I remembered that

141
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:10,390
differently, but I see what you're

142
00:04:10,390 --> 00:04:11,830
saying”? Can they admit that they

143
00:04:11,830 --> 00:04:14,230
were mistaken? Or does every conversation

144
00:04:14,230 --> 00:04:16,390
become another reason you should distrust

145
00:04:16,390 --> 00:04:19,110
yourself? Because healthy relationships don't

146
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:20,390
require perfect memory.

147
00:04:20,535 --> 00:04:23,095
They require honesty, accountability, and a

148
00:04:23,095 --> 00:04:24,615
willingness to correct mistakes.

149
00:04:24,855 --> 00:04:26,695
Now let's look at another pattern.

150
00:04:26,775 --> 00:04:29,655
Love-bombing. Imagine meeting someone new.

151
00:04:29,655 --> 00:04:31,175
Within days, they're telling you that

152
00:04:31,175 --> 00:04:32,695
you're unlike anyone they've ever met.

153
00:04:32,850 --> 00:04:34,370
They message you constantly.

154
00:04:34,370 --> 00:04:35,650
They want to know everything about

155
00:04:35,650 --> 00:04:37,330
you. They talk about your future

156
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:39,410
together. They make enormous promises.

157
00:04:39,650 --> 00:04:40,930
Maybe they send gifts.

158
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:42,370
Maybe they tell you they've never

159
00:04:42,370 --> 00:04:43,490
felt this way before.

160
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:44,290
And at first...

161
00:04:44,290 --> 00:04:45,330
it feels incredible.

162
00:04:45,330 --> 00:04:46,290
You feel seen.

163
00:04:46,290 --> 00:04:47,090
You feel wanted.

164
00:04:47,465 --> 00:04:48,985
You feel like you've finally met

165
00:04:48,985 --> 00:04:50,585
someone who understands you.

166
00:04:50,665 --> 00:04:52,025
But here's the important part.

167
00:04:52,105 --> 00:04:54,025
Intense affection is not automatically

168
00:04:54,025 --> 00:04:56,345
manipulation. Some people are naturally

169
00:04:56,345 --> 00:04:58,985
expressive. Some relationships develop quickly

170
00:04:58,985 --> 00:05:00,185
without becoming unhealthy.

171
00:05:00,470 --> 00:05:02,630
The concern begins when overwhelming affection

172
00:05:02,630 --> 00:05:04,870
is used to rush intimacy, bypass

173
00:05:04,870 --> 00:05:06,790
your boundaries, or create pressure and

174
00:05:06,790 --> 00:05:09,270
obligation. Imagine you tell this person...

175
00:05:09,430 --> 00:05:10,710
“I really like you, but I

176
00:05:10,710 --> 00:05:12,150
want to take things slowly.”

177
00:05:12,310 --> 00:05:13,510
Instead of respecting that...

178
00:05:13,510 --> 00:05:14,310
they become upset.

179
00:05:14,585 --> 00:05:15,865
They say you must not care

180
00:05:15,865 --> 00:05:16,825
about them as much as they

181
00:05:16,825 --> 00:05:17,785
care about you.

182
00:05:17,865 --> 00:05:19,385
They remind you of everything they've

183
00:05:19,385 --> 00:05:20,105
done for you.

184
00:05:20,185 --> 00:05:21,145
They tell you that if you

185
00:05:21,145 --> 00:05:22,745
truly loved them, you wouldn't need

186
00:05:22,745 --> 00:05:25,305
time. Suddenly... your boundary becomes evidence

187
00:05:25,305 --> 00:05:26,505
that you're hurting them.

188
00:05:26,665 --> 00:05:27,865
And the affection that felt like

189
00:05:27,865 --> 00:05:29,545
a gift... starts feeling like a

190
00:05:29,545 --> 00:05:32,070
debt. Later, perhaps after a disagreement,

191
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:33,510
their warmth disappears.

192
00:05:33,670 --> 00:05:34,790
They become distant.

193
00:05:34,870 --> 00:05:36,150
They withdraw attention.

194
00:05:36,390 --> 00:05:38,070
Then, when you start pulling away,

195
00:05:38,070 --> 00:05:39,350
the affection returns.

196
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:41,110
The grand promises come back.

197
00:05:41,190 --> 00:05:42,470
The apologies come back.

198
00:05:42,905 --> 00:05:44,265
And you start hoping that the

199
00:05:44,265 --> 00:05:46,025
wonderful version of the relationship will

200
00:05:46,025 --> 00:05:48,745
return permanently. This push and pull

201
00:05:48,745 --> 00:05:50,985
can become emotionally exhausting.

202
00:05:51,065 --> 00:05:53,465
You stop asking whether the relationship

203
00:05:53,465 --> 00:05:54,585
is healthy overall.

204
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:56,920
Instead, you focus on getting back

205
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:58,040
to the good moments.

206
00:05:58,200 --> 00:05:59,320
So remember this.

207
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:01,640
Don't judge a relationship only by

208
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:03,640
how intensely someone pursues you in

209
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:05,560
the beginning. Watch how they respond

210
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:06,600
when you say no.

211
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:08,920
Watch whether their promises match their

212
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:11,135
behavior. Watch whether they respect your

213
00:06:11,135 --> 00:06:13,135
pace... even when it isn't what

214
00:06:13,135 --> 00:06:15,775
they wanted. Because genuine affection doesn't

215
00:06:15,775 --> 00:06:17,375
need to rush you into surrendering

216
00:06:17,375 --> 00:06:19,695
your boundaries. Now, let's talk about

217
00:06:19,695 --> 00:06:22,335
narcissistic traits. You've probably heard people

218
00:06:22,335 --> 00:06:25,055
online describe almost every selfish, rude,

219
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:27,360
or difficult person as a narcissist.

220
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:28,480
But that's not how the term

221
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:29,360
should be used.

222
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:31,040
Someone can be selfish in a

223
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:33,680
particular situation without having narcissistic

224
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:36,160
personality disorder. Someone can struggle with

225
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:39,215
empathy, become defensive, or crave attention

226
00:06:39,215 --> 00:06:40,895
without meeting the criteria for a

227
00:06:40,895 --> 00:06:44,015
clinical diagnosis. Narcissistic personality disorder

228
00:06:44,015 --> 00:06:46,415
is a complex mental health condition

229
00:06:46,415 --> 00:06:47,935
that can only be assessed by

230
00:06:47,935 --> 00:06:49,295
a qualified professional.

231
00:06:49,455 --> 00:06:51,375
So rather than diagnosing someone from

232
00:06:51,375 --> 00:06:52,255
a few interactions...

233
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:54,320
focus on the behaviors you can

234
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:56,240
actually observe. For example...

235
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:58,720
Does this person consistently expect special

236
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:00,640
treatment? Do they struggle to acknowledge

237
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:02,240
your needs when those needs conflict

238
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:04,000
with theirs? Do they take credit

239
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,240
for shared achievements but avoid responsibility

240
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:07,120
when things go wrong?

241
00:07:07,475 --> 00:07:09,475
Do they respond to reasonable criticism

242
00:07:09,475 --> 00:07:12,035
with humiliation, blame, or retaliation?

243
00:07:12,275 --> 00:07:13,875
And perhaps most importantly...

244
00:07:13,875 --> 00:07:15,235
can they recognize that other people

245
00:07:15,235 --> 00:07:17,315
have feelings, needs, and perspectives that

246
00:07:17,315 --> 00:07:18,595
matter just as much as their

247
00:07:18,595 --> 00:07:20,835
own? One incident doesn't tell you

248
00:07:20,835 --> 00:07:21,955
everything about a person.

249
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:24,320
Everyone can be selfish sometimes.

250
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:26,080
Everyone can become defensive.

251
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:27,840
What matters is the pattern.

252
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,280
And what happens when the pattern

253
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:30,640
is brought to their attention.

254
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:31,760
Can they reflect?

255
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:33,840
Can they apologize without immediately blaming

256
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:35,440
you? Can they make a genuine

257
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:36,080
effort to change?

258
00:07:36,525 --> 00:07:38,045
Or does every attempt to address

259
00:07:38,045 --> 00:07:39,725
the problem become another argument about

260
00:07:39,725 --> 00:07:41,085
why you're the real problem?

261
00:07:41,245 --> 00:07:42,445
You don't need to assign someone

262
00:07:42,445 --> 00:07:44,445
a psychological label before deciding that

263
00:07:44,445 --> 00:07:45,965
a behavior is unacceptable.

264
00:07:46,205 --> 00:07:47,805
You can recognize that a relationship

265
00:07:47,805 --> 00:07:48,525
is hurting you...

266
00:07:48,525 --> 00:07:50,445
without pretending to know exactly what's

267
00:07:50,445 --> 00:07:51,965
happening inside the other person's mind.

268
00:07:52,230 --> 00:07:53,990
Now here's where healthy boundaries become

269
00:07:53,990 --> 00:07:55,750
important. A boundary is not a

270
00:07:55,750 --> 00:07:57,750
secret trick for controlling another person.

271
00:07:57,910 --> 00:07:58,870
It's not a way to punish

272
00:07:58,870 --> 00:08:00,310
someone for disappointing you.

273
00:08:00,470 --> 00:08:01,590
And it's not a demand that

274
00:08:01,590 --> 00:08:03,750
everyone behave exactly as you prefer.

275
00:08:03,910 --> 00:08:05,510
A healthy boundary explains what you

276
00:08:05,510 --> 00:08:06,390
are willing to accept...

277
00:08:06,785 --> 00:08:07,905
and what you will do to

278
00:08:07,905 --> 00:08:09,425
protect your own well-being if that

279
00:08:09,425 --> 00:08:10,705
boundary isn't respected.

280
00:08:10,785 --> 00:08:12,865
For example... You can say, “I'm

281
00:08:12,865 --> 00:08:14,385
willing to discuss this, but I'm

282
00:08:14,385 --> 00:08:15,905
not willing to be insulted.

283
00:08:15,905 --> 00:08:17,505
If we keep insulting each other,

284
00:08:17,505 --> 00:08:19,105
I'm going to end this conversation

285
00:08:19,105 --> 00:08:20,465
and return to it when we're

286
00:08:20,465 --> 00:08:22,220
calmer.” Notice the difference.

287
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:23,260
You're not saying...

288
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:25,260
“You're not allowed to feel angry.”

289
00:08:25,420 --> 00:08:27,500
You're saying... “You can feel angry,

290
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:28,620
but I'm not going to continue

291
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:30,620
a conversation where I'm being insulted.”

292
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:31,980
The other person still has their

293
00:08:31,980 --> 00:08:33,900
feelings. They still have their opinions.

294
00:08:34,265 --> 00:08:35,385
They still have the right to

295
00:08:35,385 --> 00:08:36,825
disagree. But you also have the

296
00:08:36,825 --> 00:08:38,025
right to decide what you will

297
00:08:38,025 --> 00:08:39,705
participate in. That's what makes a

298
00:08:39,705 --> 00:08:41,305
boundary different from control.

299
00:08:41,465 --> 00:08:43,625
Control tries to dictate another person's

300
00:08:43,625 --> 00:08:45,945
choices. A boundary clarifies your own.

301
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:48,160
And sometimes... the hardest part isn't

302
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:49,200
stating the boundary.

303
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:50,400
It's following through.

304
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:51,520
You might say that you won't

305
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:53,440
continue an insulting conversation...

306
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:54,880
then remain in it for another

307
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:56,320
two hours because you're afraid of

308
00:08:56,320 --> 00:08:57,600
upsetting the other person.

309
00:08:57,760 --> 00:08:58,880
You might say that you need

310
00:08:58,880 --> 00:08:59,680
time to think...

311
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:01,280
then agree to something because they

312
00:09:01,280 --> 00:09:02,080
keep pressuring you.

313
00:09:02,345 --> 00:09:03,785
You might promise yourself that you

314
00:09:03,785 --> 00:09:05,705
won't accept a certain behavior again...

315
00:09:05,785 --> 00:09:07,145
then ignore it because things feel

316
00:09:07,145 --> 00:09:08,905
good when the conflict disappears.

317
00:09:09,065 --> 00:09:10,425
This doesn't mean you're weak.

318
00:09:10,425 --> 00:09:12,585
Boundaries can be difficult, especially when

319
00:09:12,585 --> 00:09:14,105
you care about someone or depend

320
00:09:14,105 --> 00:09:15,520
on them. But the more clearly

321
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:17,200
you understand your own limits...

322
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:18,560
the easier it becomes to make

323
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:20,640
decisions based on your values rather

324
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:21,920
than immediate pressure.

325
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,680
There's another important distinction.

326
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:25,920
Healthy people can still disappoint you.

327
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:27,440
They can misunderstand you.

328
00:09:27,440 --> 00:09:28,880
They can become defensive.

329
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:30,400
They can make mistakes that hurt.

330
00:09:30,775 --> 00:09:32,615
A healthy relationship isn't one where

331
00:09:32,615 --> 00:09:34,135
nobody ever gets upset.

332
00:09:34,135 --> 00:09:35,575
It's one where both people can

333
00:09:35,575 --> 00:09:37,255
work through those moments without one

334
00:09:37,255 --> 00:09:39,415
person consistently having to abandon

335
00:09:39,415 --> 00:09:41,735
themselves. Imagine telling a friend...

336
00:09:41,735 --> 00:09:42,855
“I can't help you with this

337
00:09:42,855 --> 00:09:44,135
today. I'm exhausted.”

338
00:09:44,490 --> 00:09:46,730
A healthy response might be disappointment.

339
00:09:46,890 --> 00:09:48,490
They might wish you were available.

340
00:09:48,570 --> 00:09:49,610
They might ask if you can

341
00:09:49,610 --> 00:09:50,650
help another day.

342
00:09:50,650 --> 00:09:52,090
But they can still respect your

343
00:09:52,090 --> 00:09:54,810
answer. An unhealthy response might be...

344
00:09:54,890 --> 00:09:56,330
“Wow. I guess I know how

345
00:09:56,330 --> 00:09:57,290
little I matter to you.”

346
00:09:57,685 --> 00:09:59,765
Or... “After everything I've done for

347
00:09:59,765 --> 00:10:01,285
you, this is how you treat

348
00:10:01,285 --> 00:10:03,205
me?” The issue isn't that they

349
00:10:03,205 --> 00:10:05,685
feel disappointed. They're allowed to feel

350
00:10:05,685 --> 00:10:07,925
disappointed. The issue is when their

351
00:10:07,925 --> 00:10:09,925
disappointment becomes a tool for making

352
00:10:09,925 --> 00:10:12,005
you feel guilty enough to surrender

353
00:10:12,005 --> 00:10:13,880
your decision. And this can happen

354
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:17,960
in friendships, romantic relationships, families, and

355
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:21,080
workplaces. Sometimes it happens intentionally.

356
00:10:21,320 --> 00:10:23,400
Sometimes people have learned unhealthy ways

357
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:25,640
of communicating and repeat them without

358
00:10:25,640 --> 00:10:27,400
fully recognizing what they're doing.

359
00:10:27,815 --> 00:10:29,335
You don't always need to determine

360
00:10:29,335 --> 00:10:31,175
their intentions before responding to the

361
00:10:31,175 --> 00:10:33,655
behavior. You can acknowledge their feelings...

362
00:10:33,655 --> 00:10:36,135
without accepting responsibility for every feeling

363
00:10:36,135 --> 00:10:38,295
they experience. You can care about

364
00:10:38,295 --> 00:10:40,535
someone... without agreeing to everything they

365
00:10:40,535 --> 00:10:42,530
ask. And you can love someone...

366
00:10:42,530 --> 00:10:44,770
while recognizing that a particular relationship

367
00:10:44,770 --> 00:10:46,850
dynamic is not healthy for you.

368
00:10:47,010 --> 00:10:48,210
So how do you recognize the

369
00:10:48,210 --> 00:10:49,890
difference between an ordinary relationship

370
00:10:49,890 --> 00:10:51,970
problem and a more serious pattern

371
00:10:51,970 --> 00:10:54,385
of manipulation? Start by asking yourself

372
00:10:54,385 --> 00:10:55,265
a few questions.

373
00:10:55,505 --> 00:10:57,425
First... Can you say no without

374
00:10:57,425 --> 00:10:59,475
being repeatedly punished, threatened, or

375
00:10:59,425 --> 00:11:01,185
pressured into changing your answer?

376
00:11:01,345 --> 00:11:03,265
Second... Can you express a concern

377
00:11:03,265 --> 00:11:05,185
without the conversation always turning into

378
00:11:05,185 --> 00:11:06,465
an attack on your character?

379
00:11:06,625 --> 00:11:08,800
Third... When someone hurts you, can

380
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:10,160
they acknowledge the impact of their

381
00:11:10,160 --> 00:11:12,000
behavior, even if they didn't intend

382
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:12,800
to hurt you?

383
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:14,880
Fourth... Do their apologies lead to

384
00:11:14,880 --> 00:11:16,800
meaningful changes? Or do the same

385
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:18,720
actions keep happening, followed by the

386
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:20,560
same explanations and promises?

387
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:22,525
And finally... Do you feel free

388
00:11:22,525 --> 00:11:24,605
to have your own opinions, friendships,

389
00:11:24,605 --> 00:11:26,125
interests, and decisions?

390
00:11:26,285 --> 00:11:27,805
Or have you gradually started making

391
00:11:27,805 --> 00:11:29,085
your world smaller...

392
00:11:29,165 --> 00:11:30,765
because you're afraid of how someone

393
00:11:30,765 --> 00:11:33,245
will react? No single answer proves

394
00:11:33,245 --> 00:11:34,685
that someone is manipulating you.

395
00:11:34,970 --> 00:11:36,810
But repeated patterns can tell you

396
00:11:36,810 --> 00:11:38,570
something important about the relationship.

397
00:11:38,730 --> 00:11:40,170
Especially when those patterns leave you

398
00:11:40,170 --> 00:11:43,290
feeling afraid, isolated, constantly guilty, or

399
00:11:43,290 --> 00:11:45,050
unable to trust your own judgment.

400
00:11:45,050 --> 00:11:46,410
And if you recognize yourself in

401
00:11:46,410 --> 00:11:48,810
these examples... please don't immediately blame

402
00:11:48,810 --> 00:11:50,490
yourself for not noticing sooner.

403
00:11:50,765 --> 00:11:52,765
Manipulation isn't always obvious.

404
00:11:52,925 --> 00:11:55,165
It can exist alongside genuine affection.

405
00:11:55,245 --> 00:11:56,525
Someone can be kind on Monday...

406
00:11:56,525 --> 00:11:58,125
and dismissive on Tuesday.

407
00:11:58,285 --> 00:12:00,045
They can apologize sincerely in one

408
00:12:00,045 --> 00:12:01,565
moment... and repeat the same behavior

409
00:12:01,565 --> 00:12:03,165
later. You may remember the good

410
00:12:03,165 --> 00:12:04,765
parts of the relationship and struggle

411
00:12:04,765 --> 00:12:06,205
to reconcile them with the painful

412
00:12:06,205 --> 00:12:08,260
parts. That confusion doesn't mean you're

413
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:09,940
foolish. It means you're trying to

414
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:11,780
understand a complicated situation.

415
00:12:11,940 --> 00:12:13,140
You also don't have to confront

416
00:12:13,140 --> 00:12:14,740
someone immediately to prove that you're

417
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:16,420
strong. If you're worried about how

418
00:12:16,420 --> 00:12:18,420
they'll react, take time to think.

419
00:12:18,420 --> 00:12:19,860
Talk to someone you trust.

420
00:12:19,860 --> 00:12:20,980
Write down what has been happening.

421
00:12:21,285 --> 00:12:23,205
If you feel controlled, threatened, or

422
00:12:23,205 --> 00:12:25,445
unsafe, prioritize your safety and seek

423
00:12:25,445 --> 00:12:27,925
appropriate support before setting boundaries that

424
00:12:27,925 --> 00:12:29,125
might increase the risk.

425
00:12:29,205 --> 00:12:30,565
And remember... you don't need to

426
00:12:30,565 --> 00:12:31,525
win every argument.

427
00:12:31,525 --> 00:12:32,565
You don't need to force someone

428
00:12:32,565 --> 00:12:34,085
to admit that they manipulated you.

429
00:12:34,420 --> 00:12:36,660
Sometimes, the most useful question isn't...

430
00:12:36,660 --> 00:12:37,780
“How do I make this person

431
00:12:37,780 --> 00:12:39,700
understand?” It's... “What do I need

432
00:12:39,700 --> 00:12:40,980
to do to protect my well-being

433
00:12:40,980 --> 00:12:42,500
if this behavior continues?”

434
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:44,020
Because the purpose of understanding

435
00:12:44,020 --> 00:12:45,700
manipulation isn't to learn how to

436
00:12:45,700 --> 00:12:46,660
manipulate people back.

437
00:12:46,845 --> 00:12:48,525
It's not to start analyzing every

438
00:12:48,525 --> 00:12:50,685
message... or searching for hidden meanings

439
00:12:50,685 --> 00:12:52,045
in every disagreement.

440
00:12:52,125 --> 00:12:53,405
And it's certainly not to assume

441
00:12:53,405 --> 00:12:55,005
that everyone who hurts your feelings

442
00:12:55,005 --> 00:12:57,005
is secretly trying to control you.

443
00:12:57,165 --> 00:12:58,685
The purpose is to recognize the

444
00:12:58,685 --> 00:13:00,445
difference between a relationship where both

445
00:13:00,445 --> 00:13:01,965
people have room to be human...

446
00:13:02,210 --> 00:13:04,290
and one where one person's needs,

447
00:13:04,290 --> 00:13:06,770
feelings, or control consistently take priority

448
00:13:06,770 --> 00:13:07,730
over the other's.

449
00:13:07,810 --> 00:13:10,050
Healthy boundaries don't eliminate conflict.

450
00:13:10,130 --> 00:13:11,570
They make room for honesty.

451
00:13:11,650 --> 00:13:13,330
Healthy affection doesn't require you to

452
00:13:13,330 --> 00:13:14,370
abandon your independence.

453
00:13:14,685 --> 00:13:16,845
It allows closeness without ownership.

454
00:13:16,925 --> 00:13:18,845
And healthy relationships don't demand that

455
00:13:18,845 --> 00:13:20,365
you prove your loyalty by ignoring

456
00:13:20,365 --> 00:13:21,485
your own discomfort.

457
00:13:21,565 --> 00:13:22,845
They allow both people to grow...

458
00:13:22,845 --> 00:13:24,845
without one person having to become

459
00:13:24,845 --> 00:13:26,285
smaller for the other to feel

460
00:13:26,285 --> 00:13:28,125
powerful. So the next time someone

461
00:13:28,125 --> 00:13:29,485
makes you question your feelings...

462
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:31,380
pause before assuming that you're the

463
00:13:31,380 --> 00:13:33,140
problem. Look at the pattern.

464
00:13:33,220 --> 00:13:34,340
Look at the willingness to take

465
00:13:34,340 --> 00:13:36,420
responsibility. Look at what happens when

466
00:13:36,420 --> 00:13:38,100
you express a reasonable boundary.

467
00:13:38,180 --> 00:13:40,020
And remember... you can be compassionate

468
00:13:40,020 --> 00:13:41,540
without being endlessly available.

469
00:13:41,865 --> 00:13:43,705
You can forgive someone without pretending

470
00:13:43,705 --> 00:13:45,705
nothing happened. You can understand why

471
00:13:45,705 --> 00:13:47,465
someone behaves a certain way...

472
00:13:47,465 --> 00:13:49,145
without accepting that behavior in your

473
00:13:49,145 --> 00:13:50,745
life. And you can care deeply

474
00:13:50,745 --> 00:13:51,865
about another person...

475
00:13:51,945 --> 00:13:53,945
without giving them unlimited access to

476
00:13:53,945 --> 00:13:55,625
your time, your choices, or your

477
00:13:55,625 --> 00:13:56,185
sense of self.

478
00:13:56,500 --> 00:13:58,580
Because a healthy relationship shouldn't require

479
00:13:58,580 --> 00:14:00,260
you to stop trusting yourself...

480
00:14:00,260 --> 00:14:01,620
in order to keep someone else

481
00:14:01,620 --> 00:14:03,940
comfortable. You deserve room to speak.

482
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:05,220
Room to disagree.

483
00:14:05,300 --> 00:14:06,420
Room to say no.

484
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:07,860
And room to be yourself...

485
00:14:07,940 --> 00:14:09,860
without constantly fearing what will happen

486
00:14:09,860 --> 00:14:10,260
when you are.

487
00:14:10,545 --> 00:14:12,545
Think deeper. Live better.

488
00:14:12,545 --> 00:14:14,465
Become more. This is Why We

489
00:14:14,465 --> 00:14:14,865
Become.

